# Backlog Summary (Future Development & Improvements)

Even after the core reboot, there are significant areas to expand and refine. The following are key items in the development backlog, drawn from remaining TODOs and planned enhancements:

*   **Multi-Jurisdiction Expansion:** Continue adding support for more courts beyond the initial set. Brazil has dozens of state and federal courts; each one using PJe can be integrated with minimal effort once the framework is in place. A multi-tribunal adapter guide is provided to streamline this. **Backlog:** Create adapters/config for at least 10 additional courts (e.g., TJSP, TJRJ, TRF1 etc.), aiming for broad coverage.
*   **LLM Prompt Refinement & Versioning:** Monitor the AI analysis quality and iterate on the prompt or model parameters for better accuracy. If certain legal jargon confuses the model or certain outcome types aren’t recognized, the prompt can be improved. Each change should use the prompt versioning system so we track improvements. **Backlog:** Implement a mechanism to store prompt versions in the repository and reference them in analysis records. Possibly integrate a CI check that ensures any prompt changes require a corresponding version bump.
*   **Additional LLM Providers:** Evaluate integrating other language models to reduce dependency on a single API (for cost or accuracy reasons). For example, OpenAI’s GPT-4 or an open-source model like LLaMA could be options. **Backlog:** Abstract the analyzer to easily plug in new providers (the groundwork with Pydantic-AI is laid), and test at least one alternative model on a subset of cases to compare performance. Plans for adding new LLM providers are already outlined.
*   **OpenSkill Model Tuning:** With more data coming in, revisit the rating algorithm assumptions. **Backlog:** includes a plan to refine the OpenSkill rating model (e.g., adjust volatility or consider domain-specific tweaks). We might introduce features like time-decay of ratings or separate ratings by case category. Any changes should be validated against historical data to ensure they improve the meaningfulness of rankings.
*   **Enhanced Analytics & Reports:** Leverage the growing database to implement advanced analytics features. For instance, a module for calculating court efficiency metrics (average case duration if data available), or identifying top performing lawyers in specific law areas. The plan for advanced analytics features is in place. **Backlog:** Implement at least one such feature, e.g., a monthly report that lists lawyers with the most wins or a success rate dashboard.
*   **Data Validation Layer:** Introduce a formal validation step after each pipeline stage. Plans suggest creating a validation module to enforce data quality rules (for example, no duplicate case IDs, lawyer names match a certain pattern, etc.) before data is committed to the database. This will catch anomalies (maybe from API quirks or AI errors) early.
*   **Error Handling & Logging Improvements:** Although basic logging is in place, a comprehensive audit is planned to improve how errors are handled. For instance, define custom exception classes for common issues (API timeout, analysis failure) and ensure the pipeline catches and logs them without crashing. Implement structured logging (JSON logs) for easier debugging and monitoring. **Backlog:** Apply the error handling best practices project-wide (there’s a dedicated plan for this), and add more test cases for failure scenarios to ensure the system degrades gracefully.
*   **Pipeline Performance Optimization:** As concurrency increases, there might be room to optimize the async pipeline. A plan exists to optimize the async pipeline usage of tasks and possibly introduce concurrency controls or worker pools. **Backlog:** Profile the pipeline under load and eliminate bottlenecks (e.g., too slow PDF I/O or suboptimal batch sizes). Implement improvements like processing PDFs in memory streams, or using asyncio queues to better orchestrate stages.
*   **Web Dashboard & API:** Develop the user-facing side. A web dashboard plan is outlined to present key stats via either a simple web app or API endpoints. **Backlog:** Decide on a framework (Streamlit vs FastAPI+Vue, etc.) and implement at least a read-only dashboard for monitoring (court coverage, latest rankings). Also potentially provide a public API for advanced users to query data (subject to rate limits).
*   **Documentation & Developer Experience:** Continue improving docs (some are already prepared, like best practices for Pydantic models and contributor guides). Ensure the repository remains contributor-friendly with up-to-date tutorials (there are Jupyter notebook tutorials for the pipeline and adapters). **Backlog:** Expand documentation for multi-court configuration and how external contributors can add a new tribunal adapter easily. Also, ensure the code follows the set code quality standards (e.g., Ruff, MyPy configurations are enforced in CI) – this is ongoing but must be maintained as the project grows.

These backlog items ensure that after the initial reboot launch, CausaGanha will continue to evolve. The focus will shift from core functionality (which the reboot establishes) to scaling up and refining. Regular revisiting of the MASTERPLAN and backlog will help prioritize these tasks in future sprints. Each item above either has an associated plan document or will be specified in a user story to guide implementation. By tackling these, the project can move from an alpha into a polished, fully-featured platform for legal analytics.
